  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 224 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nTuesday, we are forecast to be under a weak ridge with a digging \ntrough west of the Rocky Mountains. Temperatures are expected to be \npleasant with highs forecast in the 60s for the county warning area \n(CWA). \n\nOur region is on the back end of a weak jet streak, so we have the \npotential for breezy conditions Tuesday afternoon for the western \nCWA. Wind gusts up to 30 mph are possible, although lingering cloud \ncover from earlier precipitation could prevent stronger winds from \nmixing down. If skies completely clear, or clear earlier, we could \nsee stronger winds. Models are a bit conflicted with the wind \npotential. NBM probabilities have around a 50% chance of wind gusts \nover 35 mph for our eastern Colorado counties. LREF has \nprobabilities of wind gusts over 30 mph around 10%. \n\nConditions are forecast to remain pleasant Wednesday with partly \ncloudy skies and high temperatures forecast in the 60s across the \narea. As the day progresses, we transition to more of a southwest \nupper-level flow with a developing trough of the west coast. \n\nThursday and Friday, the forecast remains consistent with high \ntemperatures forecast in the upper 50s to low 60s. Our southwesterly \nupper-level flow will bring afternoon and overnight chances for rain \nshowers as embedded shortwaves are ejected across the region. \nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 30-40% with low QPF \namounts. \n\nLooking past Friday, we have the potential to return to more of an \nactive weather pattern depending on the track and evolution of the \naforementioned trough off the west coast.",
